      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=en.wikipedia.org&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<p><span class=\"autocomment\">Later life and legacy: </span> Added link</p>\n<table style=\"background-color: #fff; color: #202122;\">\n\t\t\t\t<col class=\"diff-marker\" />\n\t\t\t\t<col class=\"diff-content\" />\n\t\t\t\t<col class=\"diff-marker\" />\n\t\t\t\t<col class=\"diff-content\" />\n\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"diff-title\" lang=\"en\">\n\t\t\t\t<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color: #fff; color: #202122; text-align: center;\">← Previous revision</td>\n\t\t\t\t<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"background-color: #fff; color: #202122; text-align: center;\">Revision as of 12:21, 30 August 2026</td>\n\t\t\t\t</tr><tr>\n  <td class=\"diff-lineno\" colspan=\"2\">Line 27:</td>\n  <td class=\"diff-lineno\" colspan=\"2\">Line 27:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><br /></td>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><br /></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><div>===Later life and legacy===</div></td>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><div>===Later life and legacy===</div></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #ffe49c; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><div>Scott received the [[Public Health Service Distinguished Service Medal]] in 1973. She received the [[American Nurses Association]] (ANA) Honorary Recognition Award the next year. She retired from PHS in 1979 as a [[rear admiral (United States)|rear admiral]].&lt;ref name=Daughters/&gt; Scott had extensive involvement in international nursing. She worked as a consultant to international organizations in India and Israel.&lt;ref name=Papers&gt;{{cite web|title=Jessie May Scott Papers|url=http://www.nursing.upenn.edu/history/Documents/New%20and%20Revised%20Finding%20Aids%20%283.22.2011%29/Scott_JessieMay_findingaid.pdf|publisher=[[University of Pennsylvania]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014|archive-date=December 10, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141210162130/http://www.nursing.upenn.edu/history/Documents/New%20and%20Revised%20Finding%20Aids%20(3.22.2011)/Scott_JessieMay_findingaid.pdf|url-status=dead}}&lt;/ref&gt; She was named among the first group of Living Legends of the [[American Academy of Nursing]] in 1994.&lt;ref name=AAN&gt;{{cite web|title=Academy Living Legends|url=http://www.aannet.org/living-legends|publisher=[[American Academy of Nursing]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014}}&lt;/ref&gt;</div></td>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #a3d3ff; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><div>Scott received the [[Public Health Service Distinguished Service Medal]] in 1973. She received the [[American Nurses Association]] (ANA) Honorary Recognition Award the next year. She retired from PHS in 1979 as a [[rear admiral (United States)|rear admiral]].&lt;ref name=Daughters/&gt; Scott had extensive involvement in international nursing. She worked as a consultant to international organizations in India and Israel.&lt;ref name=Papers&gt;{{cite web|title=Jessie May Scott Papers|url=http://www.nursing.upenn.edu/history/Documents/New%20and%20Revised%20Finding%20Aids%20%283.22.2011%29/Scott_JessieMay_findingaid.pdf|publisher=[[University of Pennsylvania]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014|archive-date=December 10, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141210162130/http://www.nursing.upenn.edu/history/Documents/New%20and%20Revised%20Finding%20Aids%20(3.22.2011)/Scott_JessieMay_findingaid.pdf|url-status=dead}}&lt;/ref&gt; She was named among the first group of <ins style=\"font-weight: bold; text-decoration: none;\">[[List of Living Legends of the American Academy of Nursing|</ins>Living Legends<ins style=\"font-weight: bold; text-decoration: none;\">]]</ins> of the [[American Academy of Nursing]] in 1994.&lt;ref name=AAN&gt;{{cite web|title=Academy Living Legends|url=http://www.aannet.org/living-legends|publisher=[[American Academy of Nursing]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014}}&lt;/ref&gt;</div></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><br /></td>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><br /></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><div>Scott died on October 20, 2009, in hospice care at The Washington Home in [[Washington, D.C.]] She had been suffering from [[heart failure|congestive heart failure]].&lt;ref name=WP&gt;{{cite news|last1=Sullivan|first1=Patricia|title=Jessie M. Scott, 94; promoted nurse education|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2009/10/29/AR2009102904545.html|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014|date=October 30, 2009}}&lt;/ref&gt; She was posthumously inducted into the ANA Hall of Fame in 2014.&lt;ref name=HOF&gt;{{cite web|title=RADM Jessie M. Scott, DSc, RN, FAAN (1915–2009) (2014 Inductee) |url=http://www.nursingworld.org/FunctionalMenuCategories/AboutANA/Honoring-Nurses/HallofFame/2014-HOF-Inductees/Jessie-M-Scott-2014.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20141207105655/http://www.nursingworld.org/FunctionalMenuCategories/AboutANA/Honoring-Nurses/HallofFame/2014-HOF-Inductees/Jessie-M-Scott-2014.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 7, 2014 |publisher=[[American Nurses Association]] |accessdate=December 7, 2014 }}&lt;/ref&gt; The ANA awards the Jessie M. Scott Award to a nurse who exhibits work that creates links between nursing research, education and practice.&lt;ref name=Award&gt;{{cite web|title=Jessie M. Scott Award|url=http://www.nursingworld.org/FunctionalMenuCategories/AboutANA/Honoring-Nurses/NationalAwardsProgram/NatlAwards/JessieMScott|publisher=[[American Nurses Association]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014}}&lt;/ref&gt; In 1996, the award was presented to nursing theorist [[Imogene King|Imogene M. King]].&lt;ref&gt;{{Cite web |title=Imogene King Obituary (2007) - St Petersburg Beach, FL - Tampa Bay Times |url=https://www.legacy.com/amp/obituaries/sptimes/100288228 |access-date=2022-03-11 |website=www.legacy.com |language=en}}&lt;/ref&gt;</div></td>\n  <td class=\"diff-marker\"></td>\n  <td style=\"background-color: #f8f9fa; color: #202122; font-size: 88%; border-style: solid; border-width: 1px 1px 1px 4px; border-radius: 0.33em; border-color: #eaecf0; vertical-align: top; white-space: pre-wrap;\"><div>Scott died on October 20, 2009, in hospice care at The Washington Home in [[Washington, D.C.]] She had been suffering from [[heart failure|congestive heart failure]].&lt;ref name=WP&gt;{{cite news|last1=Sullivan|first1=Patricia|title=Jessie M. Scott, 94; promoted nurse education|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2009/10/29/AR2009102904545.html|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014|date=October 30, 2009}}&lt;/ref&gt; She was posthumously inducted into the ANA Hall of Fame in 2014.&lt;ref name=HOF&gt;{{cite web|title=RADM Jessie M. Scott, DSc, RN, FAAN (1915–2009) (2014 Inductee) |url=http://www.nursingworld.org/FunctionalMenuCategories/AboutANA/Honoring-Nurses/HallofFame/2014-HOF-Inductees/Jessie-M-Scott-2014.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20141207105655/http://www.nursingworld.org/FunctionalMenuCategories/AboutANA/Honoring-Nurses/HallofFame/2014-HOF-Inductees/Jessie-M-Scott-2014.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 7, 2014 |publisher=[[American Nurses Association]] |accessdate=December 7, 2014 }}&lt;/ref&gt; The ANA awards the Jessie M. Scott Award to a nurse who exhibits work that creates links between nursing research, education and practice.&lt;ref name=Award&gt;{{cite web|title=Jessie M. Scott Award|url=http://www.nursingworld.org/FunctionalMenuCategories/AboutANA/Honoring-Nurses/NationalAwardsProgram/NatlAwards/JessieMScott|publisher=[[American Nurses Association]]|accessdate=December 7, 2014}}&lt;/ref&gt; In 1996, the award was presented to nursing theorist [[Imogene King|Imogene M. King]].&lt;ref&gt;{{Cite web |title=Imogene King Obituary (2007) - St Petersburg Beach, FL - Tampa Bay Times |url=https://www.legacy.com/amp/obituaries/sptimes/100288228 |access-date=2022-03-11 |website=www.legacy.com |language=en}}&lt;/ref&gt;</div></td>\n</tr>\n\n<!-- diff cache key enwiki:diff:1.41:old-1337954980:rev-1372120314:wikidiff2=table:1.14.1:ff290eae -->\n</table>",
